   "text": "I read the drawing as a cyclic depsipeptide that looks like a didemnin analogue. I wrote the SMILES by following the chain from the pyruvyl-proline tail around the macrocycle.\n\n**Side chain (left to right in the drawing):**\n- A pyruvyl group, CH3C(=O)C(=O)\u2013, is attached to the nitrogen of a proline ring.\n- That proline is bonded through its carbonyl to an N-methyl-leucine, which has a wedged isobutyl group.\n- The N-methyl-leucine carbonyl is bonded to the NH of a threonine-type residue, which is also part of the macrocycle.\n\n**Macrocycle, starting at the threonine \u03b1-carbon and going around:**\n1. The threonine \u03b1-carbon carries the side-chain NH (hashed bond). Its \u03b2-carbon bears a hashed methyl and the ring ester oxygen.\n2. That oxygen is esterified to the carbonyl of an N-methyl-O-methyltyrosine (4-methoxybenzyl side chain).\n3. The N-methyl-tyrosine nitrogen is acylated by a proline, whose nitrogen is in turn acylated by a leucine.\n4. The leucine NH is acylated by a CH(CH3) carbon, which is bonded to a ketone carbon.\n5. The ketone carbon is bonded to a CH carrying an isopropyl group, which bears the ester oxygen.\n6. That oxygen is esterified to a carbonyl, followed by the CH2 that the drawing labels \"C\".\n7. The CH2 is followed by CH(OH), then a CH bearing a sec-butyl group (hashed methyl) and the NH.\n8. That NH closes the ring onto the threonine carbonyl.\n\nI assigned the @/@@ marks from each wedge or hash and the neighbour order in the SMILES. The proline, tyrosine and leucine centres came out as the usual L configuration and the N-methyl-leucine in the tail as D. I did not run the string through a parser.\n\nSMILES: CC(=O)C(=O)N1CCC[C@H]1C(=O)N(C)[C@H](CC(C)C)C(=O)N[C@H]3[C@@H](C)OC(=O)[C@H](Cc4ccc(OC)cc4)N(C)C(=O)[C@@H]4CCCN4C(=O)[C@H](CC(C)C)NC(=O)[C@@H](C)C(=O)[C@H](C(C)C)OC(=O)C[C@H](O)[C@@H]([C@@H](C)CC)NC3=O"
